WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.680
So, have you ever wondered why, like, the soundtrack of a horror movie makes your heart race and your muscles just literally clench up,

00:10.100 --> 00:17.040
even when you're, you know, sitting completely safe on your couch and you know for an absolute fact that it is just a movie?

00:17.400 --> 00:21.000
Right. It's basically the ultimate biological hijack.

00:21.540 --> 00:29.720
You are completely aware the threat isn't real, but your body is just, it's preparing for a physical impact that is just never going to actually happen.

00:29.720 --> 00:34.400
Yeah. And that biological hijack is exactly our mission today.

00:34.760 --> 00:37.620
Welcome to this deep dive into the source material.

00:38.160 --> 00:44.340
We're unpacking a really highly detailed, incredibly provocative paper from November 2026.

00:44.720 --> 00:46.800
Yeah. By the independent researcher Flyxion.

00:47.080 --> 00:54.220
Exactly. Titled, Mimesis and Mechanical Coupling in the Bodily Response to Sound, an Oscillatory Account.

00:54.220 --> 01:04.320
So, our goal today is to really explore that profound separation between your brain, you know, understanding a sound and your body being physically forced by it.

01:04.780 --> 01:12.500
Because we have this deeply ingrained habit of treating hearing as pure information processing, like you're just reading text on a screen.

01:12.720 --> 01:13.380
Right. Like data.

01:13.380 --> 01:19.160
Exactly. Like the sound waves hit your ear and your brain just neutrally translates them into data about your environment.

01:19.520 --> 01:25.480
But what Flyxion's research does is, well, it tears that assumption down to the studs.

01:25.500 --> 01:26.100
It really does.

01:26.220 --> 01:30.860
It exposes the hidden biological and mechanical defense systems that your body deploys.

01:31.240 --> 01:33.820
I mean, the literal millisecond sound hits it.

01:33.820 --> 01:34.700
Which is wild.

01:35.000 --> 01:50.000
So, we're going to look at the boundaries of the famous mimetic hypothesis, break down the five distinct pathways through which sound, like, attacks and guides us, and then dive into the actual biomechanics of how your muscles act as a literal filter for sound.

01:50.260 --> 01:57.900
And we'll end with the mind-bending idea that these bodily responses might be the true origin of cultural memes, which is just fascinating.

01:58.160 --> 01:58.500
It is.

01:59.000 --> 02:03.320
So, let's start with where our traditional understanding of listening completely falls apart.

02:03.320 --> 02:12.860
Right. So, to appreciate the gap in our knowledge here, we have to look at the prevailing theory a Flyxion is building upon, which is Arnie Cox's mimetic hypothesis.

02:13.240 --> 02:13.400
Okay.

02:13.620 --> 02:16.580
This is a massive cornerstone of embodied music cognition.

02:16.580 --> 02:21.440
The core argument is that when we hear music, we aren't passively decoding acoustic data.

02:21.740 --> 02:27.280
We actually understand the music by covertly simulating the physical actions that produced it.

02:27.280 --> 02:32.360
So, if I'm, like, listening to a cello piece, I'm not just hearing the notes.

02:32.820 --> 02:40.640
My brain is, on some invisible level, imagining what it feels like to physically draw a bow across those heavy strings.

02:41.040 --> 02:41.560
Precisely.

02:42.060 --> 02:44.980
Your motor systems are engaging as if you are the performer.

02:45.320 --> 02:48.880
And there is a massive body of neuroimaging supporting this, actually.

02:49.260 --> 02:49.540
Really?

02:49.780 --> 02:49.980
Yeah.

02:49.980 --> 02:50.720
Like, physical scans.

02:51.280 --> 02:51.480
Yeah.

02:51.660 --> 03:04.480
Studies by Gazzola and Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation Studies by Fadiga, they've shown that hearing in action engages the premortar cortex in what we call a somatotopically organized way.

03:04.880 --> 03:05.540
Okay, wait.

03:06.540 --> 03:07.400
Somatotopically organized.

03:07.660 --> 03:08.860
Let me piece that together.

03:08.860 --> 03:11.080
So, somato means body.

03:11.500 --> 03:12.400
Topic means map.

03:12.980 --> 03:15.100
So, the brain maps the physical body.

03:15.740 --> 03:20.560
And the sound activates the specific map coordinates for the body part that, like, made the sound.

03:20.780 --> 03:22.080
That is a perfect translation.

03:22.300 --> 03:22.440
Yeah.

03:22.620 --> 03:22.860
Okay.

03:22.960 --> 03:32.280
If you hear a sound produced by a hand, like clapping or, you know, tapping a drum, the specific hand-related motor regions of your brain actually light up on an fMRI.

03:32.480 --> 03:32.760
Wow.

03:32.760 --> 03:39.980
And if you hear someone speaking or singing, the muscles in your tongue and larynx actually show measurable spikes in electrical excitability.

03:40.240 --> 03:44.020
You are silently, physically mirroring the action of the sound.

03:44.300 --> 03:49.200
Which is such a beautiful concept when we're talking about, like, acoustic music or conversation.

03:49.540 --> 03:51.940
We're all secretly playing along in our nervous systems.

03:52.680 --> 03:55.780
But Flyxion points out a massive blind spot here, right?

03:55.780 --> 03:56.660
A huge one, yeah.

03:56.840 --> 04:00.840
Because this theory completely hits a wall the moment we step outside of human-produced music.

04:00.840 --> 04:07.340
Like, what happens when the sound is an engineered film effect or a sudden vocal burst or just a violent impact?

04:07.820 --> 04:09.300
That is the breaking point.

04:09.800 --> 04:21.680
If you hear an explosive gunshot or a massive synthesized bass drop, you are obviously not covertly simulating the action of being a gun or being a synthesizer.

04:21.960 --> 04:25.180
You have no bodily experience of exploding gunpowder.

04:25.320 --> 04:29.120
Yet, your physical reaction to that sound happens in tens of milliseconds.

04:29.620 --> 04:30.600
Tens of milliseconds.

04:30.840 --> 04:34.440
And that speed is the whole problem for the mimetic hypothesis, isn't it?

04:34.480 --> 04:42.760
Because if I hear a gunshot, my neck clenches and my heart rate spikes way before my brain is even fully processed what the sound actually is.

04:43.340 --> 04:46.280
It is vastly faster than comprehension, yes.

04:46.700 --> 04:52.320
It takes hundreds of milliseconds, sometimes a full second, for the auditory cortex to identify a sound source,

04:52.800 --> 04:56.820
conceptually map it, and retrieve the semantic meaning of, you know, gunshot.

04:56.820 --> 05:05.640
So, if your body is flinching in 20 milliseconds, that reaction just cannot be based on imitating an action because you don't even know what the action is yet.

05:05.900 --> 05:06.340
Exactly.

05:06.340 --> 05:10.480
Which leads to this concept in the paper that I find just incredibly relatable.

05:10.700 --> 05:13.180
We can call it the mute button paradox.

05:13.580 --> 05:14.560
Oh, I love this part.

05:14.660 --> 05:14.800
Oh.

05:15.240 --> 05:16.280
And so, picture this for a second.

05:16.760 --> 05:19.500
You are watching an incredibly tense scene in a thriller.

05:20.120 --> 05:21.960
The protagonist is hiding in a closet.

05:22.820 --> 05:25.880
The killer is slowly walking across the creaky floorboards.

05:26.020 --> 05:26.860
We've all been there.

05:26.860 --> 05:31.680
And the musical score is this high, screeching, dissonant swell.

05:32.560 --> 05:34.700
You are physically suffering on the couch.

05:35.340 --> 05:36.240
Your palms are sweating.

05:36.360 --> 05:37.040
Your jaw is locked.

05:37.120 --> 05:38.280
Your breathing is super shallow.

05:38.940 --> 05:40.200
It just becomes too much.

05:40.760 --> 05:42.620
So, you grab the remote and you hit mute.

05:43.260 --> 05:46.020
And instantly, the physical suffering just vanishes.

05:46.760 --> 05:47.700
It just drops off a cliff.

05:48.280 --> 05:50.680
But think about what just happened cognitively.

05:51.100 --> 05:53.860
You are still looking at the exact same visual information, right?

05:54.280 --> 05:56.780
Your comprehension of the danger is completely intact.

05:56.860 --> 05:58.780
Yeah, you know the killer is right there.

05:59.020 --> 06:00.320
You know the protagonist is trapped.

06:00.840 --> 06:05.520
But by killing the audio, it's like you took your foot off the gas pedal of your own nervous system.

06:06.060 --> 06:10.220
You can still see the road perfectly, but you decoupled the physical acceleration.

06:10.920 --> 06:15.860
Which completely proves that the physical reaction is not simply a byproduct of the narrative information.

06:16.320 --> 06:22.100
And Flyxion uses this exact phenomenon to establish a really vital vocabulary distinction.

06:23.060 --> 06:23.160
Right.

06:23.300 --> 06:25.320
The difference between comprehension and forcing.

06:25.660 --> 06:25.800
Yes.

06:25.800 --> 06:29.340
Comprehension being the conscious identification of the source.

06:29.800 --> 06:31.880
Like knowing the killer is walking.

06:32.540 --> 06:40.680
And then forcing being the physical, neural, and like mechanical influence the sound exerts on your organism, entirely independent of its meaning.

06:41.460 --> 06:44.300
The sound wave is an active physical agent.

06:44.300 --> 06:45.840
It really is.

06:45.840 --> 06:47.700
It doesn't wait to be translated.

06:47.900 --> 06:50.220
You know, it forces a physical state upon you.

06:50.840 --> 06:57.980
And because we are not just passive lumps of clay, our bodies regulate several independent pathways to deal with that force.

06:57.980 --> 07:10.540
So if covert imitation isn't enough to explain why we jump at a jump scare, and the mute button proves that forcing is separate from comprehension, we need to look at the exact mechanisms the body is using.

07:10.600 --> 07:10.940
Exactly.

07:10.940 --> 07:14.480
And the paper identifies five distinct pathways.

07:14.480 --> 07:18.280
So let's dig into the first one, which is mechanical coupling.

07:18.280 --> 07:27.460
We often forget that the auditory system is a mechanical apparatus long before it becomes a neural computer.

07:27.620 --> 07:28.140
It's physical.

07:28.140 --> 07:30.700
It starts as pure physical engineering.

07:31.220 --> 07:36.060
Acoustic waves travel through the air, and they physically strike the tympanic membrane, the eardrum.

07:36.600 --> 07:36.740
Okay.

07:36.740 --> 07:47.600
That vibration moves the ossicles, which are the three tiniest bones in the human body, and they act as a mechanical lever system to push against the fluid inside the cochlea.

07:47.600 --> 07:56.040
And the paper details how that fluid movement creates a traveling wave along the basilar membrane, which is mechanically tuned, right?

07:56.120 --> 08:02.120
Like high frequencies peak at the stiff base, low frequencies peak at the wider, more compliant apex.

08:02.120 --> 08:07.260
Yeah, the frequency selectivity of your ear is entirely based on physical gradients.

08:07.920 --> 08:11.020
But mechanical coupling extends far beyond the inner ear.

08:11.200 --> 08:13.560
Sound waves hit your entire physical body.

08:14.080 --> 08:18.500
I love the math Flyxion uses to illustrate this, because I always wondered about this.

08:19.060 --> 08:24.520
The paper calculates the actual acoustic pressure force hitting a human body in a loud environment.

08:24.660 --> 08:30.120
So let's say you're standing in front of the speakers at a concert, and it's hitting 120 decibels.

08:30.240 --> 08:31.260
Which is ear splitting.

08:31.260 --> 08:33.880
It feels like the air is solid, right?

08:34.400 --> 08:41.040
But the actual acoustic pressure force hitting the front of a typical human torso is only about six newtons.

08:41.360 --> 08:44.620
Which is an incredibly small amount of force, honestly.

08:44.860 --> 08:48.320
Six newtons is equivalent to about 1.3 pounds of force.

08:48.460 --> 08:49.560
Wait, really?

08:49.780 --> 08:50.180
That's it.

08:50.380 --> 08:50.800
That's it.

08:51.000 --> 08:54.560
It is less than 1% of the body weight of an average adult.

08:55.080 --> 08:59.700
If someone placed a 1.3 pound book on your chest, you would barely even register the pressure.

08:59.700 --> 09:06.560
So why does 120 decibels of bass feel so overwhelming if it's literally just the weight of a paperback book?

09:07.280 --> 09:08.720
Because that force is not static.

09:09.320 --> 09:11.060
It is applied cyclically.

09:11.060 --> 09:14.820
It's an oscillating wave pushing and pulling at a specific frequency.

09:15.340 --> 09:19.740
And human tissue, organs, and the skeleton, they all have natural resonant frequencies.

09:20.540 --> 09:22.220
Oh, like when a wine glass shatters.

09:22.540 --> 09:23.380
Very similar, yeah.

09:23.800 --> 09:29.960
The trunk of the human body under a whole body vibration typically resonates between a few hertz and about 10 hertz.

09:29.960 --> 09:31.900
The resonant frequency.

09:32.220 --> 09:34.180
It's like pushing a kid on a swing, right?

09:34.240 --> 09:39.580
If you push it the exact right time, even with a tiny amount of force, the swing goes higher and higher.

09:40.020 --> 09:45.920
So the sound waves are pushing our ribcage at the exact frequency it naturally wants to vibrate.

09:46.340 --> 09:48.320
That is the mechanical coupling right there.

09:48.760 --> 09:50.460
Your body begins to ring like a bell.

09:50.460 --> 09:54.860
And this triggers mechanoreceptors in your skin, your joints, and your deeper tissues.

09:55.200 --> 09:55.300
Wow.

09:55.680 --> 10:01.000
The paper actually cites a fascinating study by Todd and Cody regarding loud techno music.

10:01.400 --> 10:07.360
They found that low frequency bass over 90 decibels actually triggers the sternocleidomastoid muscle.

10:07.840 --> 10:10.520
Which is that thick muscle running down the side of your neck, right?

10:10.520 --> 10:10.840
Exactly.

10:10.840 --> 10:13.500
But they didn't just find that the muscle twitched.

10:13.840 --> 10:21.140
They found the neural route it took, which completely blew my mind when I read it, is it didn't go through the auditory cortex.

10:21.580 --> 10:21.860
Nope.

10:22.040 --> 10:23.740
It went through the vestibular system.

10:24.460 --> 10:27.060
Yes, the balance center of the inner ear.

10:27.760 --> 10:36.880
The sacculus, which normally detects gravity and linear acceleration, is actually extremely sensitive to intense, low frequency acoustic pressure.

10:37.480 --> 10:40.320
So when the bass drops at a club and you feel it in your chest?

10:40.840 --> 10:45.780
And your neck, your experience, is literally somatosensory and vestibular.

10:46.140 --> 10:51.380
Your brain is receiving signals that your body is being physically accelerated through space.

10:51.700 --> 10:52.660
That is insane.

10:53.340 --> 10:54.880
So that's pathway number one.

10:55.220 --> 10:58.280
Sound is a physical punch that vibrates our mass.

10:59.620 --> 11:02.740
Pathway number two is the acoustic startle reflex.

11:03.140 --> 11:04.880
And this is the lightning fast defense system.

11:05.520 --> 11:10.020
Yeah, this reflex evolved entirely to bypass the slower cognitive centers of the brain.

11:10.020 --> 11:14.860
It's a brainstem reflex triggered by sounds that have a very high onset rate.

11:15.460 --> 11:23.240
Meaning a sound that spikes from zero to extremely loud in a fraction of a second, like a snapping twig or a popping balloon or an explosion.

11:23.680 --> 11:23.980
Exactly.

11:24.440 --> 11:26.280
And the circuitry is incredibly short.

11:26.420 --> 11:28.720
It's a raw ancient wire, basically.

11:28.720 --> 11:29.360
It is.

11:29.360 --> 11:39.400
The signal travels from the cochlear root neurons directly to the caudal pontine reticular nucleus in the lower brainstem and then shoots straight out to the spinal motor neurons.

11:39.740 --> 11:41.940
The entire trip takes tens of milliseconds.

11:42.720 --> 11:45.360
And the resulting physical movement is highly stereotyped, right?

11:45.440 --> 11:48.220
Like we all do the exact same thing when we startle.

11:48.320 --> 11:48.680
We do.

11:48.680 --> 11:54.760
The startle pattern always begins with a rapid eye blink, followed almost instantly by neck and shoulder Flyxion.

11:54.860 --> 11:57.020
You hunch your shoulders up to protect your neck.

11:57.640 --> 11:58.840
Like a turtle pulling into its shell.

11:59.060 --> 11:59.980
Exactly like that.

12:00.340 --> 12:06.260
And it also triggers a massive spike in autonomic arousal, dumping adrenaline and accelerating the heart rate.

12:06.620 --> 12:11.320
But Flyxion emphasizes that this isn't just a static, dumb alarm system.

12:11.900 --> 12:15.280
The reflex is modulated by what the organism is currently experiencing.

12:15.680 --> 12:16.000
Right.

12:16.160 --> 12:17.220
It's gain controlled.

12:17.220 --> 12:20.980
The volume of the reflex can be turned up or down based on your state.

12:21.540 --> 12:24.680
A classic example of this is pre-pulse inhibition.

12:24.940 --> 12:25.260
What's that?

12:25.940 --> 12:36.660
So if your ear detects a very faint, non-startling sound just milliseconds before a massive explosion, the physical flinch of your startle reflex is significantly reduced.

12:37.160 --> 12:42.360
Oh, because your nervous system gets a tiny warning tap, so it preemptively dampens the heavy machinery.

12:42.640 --> 12:43.140
Precisely.

12:43.520 --> 12:45.820
And conversely, there is affective modulation.

12:45.820 --> 12:55.400
If you are already in a state of high anxiety or fear, say you were walking through a dark alley or watching a really tense movie, the startle reflex is potentiated.

12:55.400 --> 13:04.220
So the exact same acoustic trigger will cause a much more violent physical flinch and a higher heart rate spike if you are already afraid.

13:04.220 --> 13:04.800
Yes.

13:04.800 --> 13:10.000
Your emotional state is physically dialing up the sensitivity of a primitive brainstem circuit.

13:10.680 --> 13:10.800
Okay.

13:10.940 --> 13:17.100
So we have the physical resonance of mechanical coupling and the brainstem lightning strike of the startle reflex.

13:17.860 --> 13:21.340
Pathway number three is entrainment to periodic structure.

13:21.940 --> 13:23.960
Basically, getting locked into a rhythm.

13:23.960 --> 13:27.500
And this moves us into the cognitive and neural physics of time.

13:28.100 --> 13:31.480
The paper relies on Large and Jones' theory of rhythmic attention.

13:31.680 --> 13:31.860
Right.

13:32.040 --> 13:36.080
We tend to think of human attention as a continuous spotlight that is just always on.

13:36.280 --> 13:39.420
But their theory suggests attention is oscillatory.

13:39.760 --> 13:40.240
It pulses.

13:40.520 --> 13:41.580
Like a strobe light.

13:42.260 --> 13:43.060
Kind of, yeah.

13:43.480 --> 13:47.340
And when we are exposed to a periodic structure and sound, like a steady beat,

13:47.780 --> 13:52.720
our internal neural oscillations physically tag onto those external frequencies.

13:52.720 --> 13:57.820
The paper points to EEG studies by Nozardin to prove this, which is so cool.

13:58.260 --> 14:06.240
If you hook someone up to an EEG and play a beat, you can literally watch their brainwaves phase lock to the tempo of the music.

14:07.060 --> 14:10.260
The neural firing physically aligns with the acoustic peaks.

14:10.380 --> 14:10.920
It's amazing.

14:11.800 --> 14:17.580
And Floddiksen grounds this in the mathematical theory of synchronization developed by physicists like Pekofsky.

14:17.880 --> 14:18.160
Okay.

14:18.380 --> 14:19.420
Lay that math on me.

14:19.420 --> 14:27.080
The mathematics dictate that if you have a self-sustained internal oscillator and it is subjected to a periodic external force,

14:27.140 --> 14:32.420
even a very weak one, the internal oscillator will eventually lock faces with the external force,

14:32.540 --> 14:35.240
provided the frequency difference isn't, you know, too vast.

14:35.640 --> 14:37.440
It's the classic physics demonstration, right?

14:37.740 --> 14:39.920
Where you put a bunch of metronomes on a wobbly board.

14:40.460 --> 14:44.540
They all start out ticking randomly, but the weak physical force traveling through the board

14:44.540 --> 14:47.060
eventually forces them all to tick in perfect unison.

14:47.060 --> 14:48.360
That is exactly it.

14:48.560 --> 14:52.340
And this synchronization isn't just happening in the brain's auditory centers.

14:52.740 --> 14:54.740
It heavily involves the motor system, too.

14:55.400 --> 15:01.360
There is a landmark study cited in the paper by Philip Silver and Traynor involving seven-month-old infants.

15:01.520 --> 15:02.640
Oh, I love this part.

15:02.900 --> 15:06.580
They took these infants and played an ambiguous rhythmic pattern for them,

15:06.580 --> 15:10.120
but while the music played, an adult physically bounced the infant.

15:10.500 --> 15:10.800
Right.

15:11.540 --> 15:14.700
And half the infants were bounced on every second beat, like a march,

15:15.300 --> 15:18.180
and the other half were bounced on every third beat, like a waltz.

15:18.460 --> 15:22.180
And when they tested the infants later to see how they actually perceived the rhythm,

15:22.740 --> 15:26.880
the infants' brains had locked onto the meter they were physically bounced to.

15:26.880 --> 15:31.600
The gravitational and vestibular acceleration of their physical body

15:31.600 --> 15:35.560
actually shaped the neural perception of the auditory pattern.

15:36.120 --> 15:38.880
That is, I mean, they didn't just hear the beat.

15:39.060 --> 15:41.500
Their bodies learned to physically inhabit the beat.

15:41.940 --> 15:42.500
Exactly.

15:42.860 --> 15:46.620
Which brings us to pathway four, covert mimetic simulation.

15:47.140 --> 15:49.340
This is Arnie Cox's theory from the beginning,

15:49.560 --> 15:53.940
but Fleckson rescues it by splitting it into two distinct types of imitation.

15:53.940 --> 15:57.020
Because as we established earlier,

15:57.440 --> 16:02.540
you cannot covertly imitate the physical action of a synthesizer or an explosion.

16:03.400 --> 16:06.220
So Fleckson argues that mimesis works on two levels.

16:06.800 --> 16:08.360
The first level is source imitation.

16:08.780 --> 16:10.380
Imitating how a sound is made.

16:10.500 --> 16:10.620
Right.

16:10.840 --> 16:12.360
You hear a piano key being struck,

16:12.520 --> 16:15.860
you subtly map the motor action of a finger striking a key.

16:16.220 --> 16:19.580
But the second level is contour imitation,

16:20.280 --> 16:23.100
embodying how the sound unfolds in time and energy.

16:23.100 --> 16:24.160
Oh, okay.

16:24.900 --> 16:26.900
So this is an analog of the dynamics.

16:27.600 --> 16:30.200
You aren't imitating the object that made the sound.

16:30.460 --> 16:34.440
You are translating the acoustic shape into a physical motor shape.

16:34.700 --> 16:35.040
Yes.

16:35.740 --> 16:37.360
Think of a staccato sound.

16:37.860 --> 16:40.760
Sharp, abrupt, disconnected bursts of noise.

16:41.400 --> 16:46.380
Your motor system reconstructs that as short, jerky, inhibited motor impulses.

16:46.720 --> 16:50.580
And a legato sound, like a long, smooth, continuous drone.

16:50.580 --> 16:55.160
A vibrato sound is reconstructed as smooth, continuous muscular extensions.

16:55.980 --> 17:00.140
A vibrato sound is reconstructed as a rapid muscular oscillation.

17:00.520 --> 17:04.320
You are literally using the tension and release of your own musculature

17:04.320 --> 17:07.060
to map the acoustic envelope of the sound wave.

17:07.060 --> 17:08.160
That is so wild.

17:08.600 --> 17:13.160
Which brings us to the final pathway, pathway five, learned anticipation.

17:13.920 --> 17:14.020
Right.

17:14.100 --> 17:17.080
This is where cultural learning and prior exposure come into play,

17:17.580 --> 17:21.540
specifically relying on David Huron's work regarding musical expectation.

17:21.900 --> 17:22.160
Okay.

17:22.540 --> 17:24.900
When you understand the syntactic rules of a genre,

17:25.180 --> 17:28.060
whether it's classical music or horror film sound design,

17:28.580 --> 17:32.820
your body begins to react before the acoustic event even happens.

17:32.820 --> 17:37.160
Is it the unbearable tension during the dead silence right before the monster attacks?

17:37.480 --> 17:38.000
Exactly.

17:38.580 --> 17:42.600
Your skin conductance rises, your neck tenses, your breathing gets shallow,

17:42.780 --> 17:47.040
entirely based on the anticipation of the forcing that you know is coming.

17:47.460 --> 17:47.760
Okay.

17:47.840 --> 17:49.820
So I'm looking at this list of five pathways.

17:50.500 --> 17:53.260
And I was struggling with a logistical problem here when I read it.

17:53.260 --> 17:53.480
What's that?

17:53.860 --> 18:00.860
So mechanical coupling, startle reflex, entrainment, mimetic simulation, learned anticipation.

18:00.860 --> 18:09.400
If I'm in an IMAX theater and the score drops a massive, heavy, orchestral drum hit at the climax of a scene,

18:09.880 --> 18:14.380
the sound has a sudden onset, it has a massive, low-frequency bass tone,

18:14.840 --> 18:19.000
it is rhythmically placed, I can identify it as a drum being struck,

18:19.520 --> 18:22.600
and the scene built up to it, so I anticipated it.

18:22.660 --> 18:22.860
Right.

18:24.080 --> 18:28.040
Aren't all five of these biological levers being pulled at the exact same millisecond?

18:28.040 --> 18:35.480
Like, how can the author claim they are biologically distinct systems if they all fire simultaneously in the real world?

18:35.480 --> 18:43.160
That is the exact challenge that is addressed by the whole system.

18:43.620 --> 18:45.080
Flechtesian addresses this, actually.

18:45.240 --> 18:49.860
So in the real world environments, you know, in real world, the environments, they overlap.

18:50.380 --> 18:51.040
They massively do.

18:51.240 --> 18:57.260
But we can isolate them in kind of like a laboratory setting by manipulating habituation and acoustic properties.

18:57.260 --> 19:01.720
So the startle reflex versus the mimetic simulation, okay?

19:01.960 --> 19:02.300
How so?

19:02.800 --> 19:04.560
All right, so like, let's take that.

19:04.820 --> 19:09.220
So if I put you in a lab and I play a recording of kind of like a terrifying abrupt gunshot,

19:09.700 --> 19:17.220
your startle pathway fires, your mimetic pathway fires as you're processing kind of like the violence of the contour of the sound.

19:17.220 --> 19:26.200
But if I play that exact same gunshot 20 times in a row with a 10-second gap between each one, your brainstem will habituate.

19:26.640 --> 19:31.540
By like the 20th gunshot, your acoustic startle reflex will like barely register.

19:31.660 --> 19:33.140
You will literally stop flinching.

19:33.680 --> 19:37.740
Because the primitive brainstem realizes it's not actually dying, so it turns the alarm off.

19:37.960 --> 19:38.320
Exactly.

19:38.320 --> 19:46.520
But the mimetic pathway, your cognitive motor mapping of the aggressive explosive shape of that sound, does not habituate in the same way.

19:46.980 --> 19:49.500
The neural representation of the force remains.

19:50.100 --> 19:54.020
You have successfully decoupled the reflex from the cognitive mapping.

19:54.360 --> 19:54.740
Oh, wow.

19:55.340 --> 19:59.980
Or we could isolate the mechanical pathway by removing the sudden onset, right?

20:00.700 --> 20:06.480
Like, if we slowly fade in a heavy bass drone over 30 seconds, there is zero starter reflex.

20:06.700 --> 20:08.200
There's no abrupt attack.

20:08.760 --> 20:12.520
But the mechanical coupling and entrainment are firing at maximum capacity.

20:12.880 --> 20:13.360
Precisely.

20:14.100 --> 20:19.960
They are independent systems, driven by different acoustic parameters, operating on vastly different timescales.

20:20.380 --> 20:25.760
And this complex assault on the nervous system demands an equally complex defense mechanism from the body.

20:26.280 --> 20:27.700
So how does the body fight back?

20:28.220 --> 20:32.200
Which brings us to the concept of the mechanical plant and oscillator storage.

20:32.200 --> 20:38.860
This is where the paper kind of leaves neuroscience and enters the actual physics of flesh, bone, and tension.

20:39.560 --> 20:43.120
And Flyxion makes a vital distinction here between attenuation and regulation.

20:43.660 --> 20:48.160
When we are subjected to a painful sound, our first instinct is attenuation.

20:48.160 --> 20:50.100
We try to change the stimulus.

20:50.320 --> 20:53.160
We plug our ears, we walk out of the room, or we hit the mute button.

20:53.480 --> 20:55.620
We physically block the waves from entering the system.

20:55.860 --> 20:56.040
Right.

20:56.320 --> 21:02.980
But if you are strapped into a theater seat or, you know, standing in a crowded subway station, attenuation is often impossible.

21:03.200 --> 21:04.820
You cannot stop the force.

21:04.820 --> 21:07.220
So the body must rely on regulation.

21:07.940 --> 21:09.260
And regulation means what, exactly?

21:09.860 --> 21:15.940
It means altering your own physical transfer function to manage how the acoustic energy travels through your mass.

21:16.220 --> 21:17.500
Altering the transfer function.

21:17.720 --> 21:18.060
Mm-hmm.

21:18.600 --> 21:18.840
Okay.

21:19.020 --> 21:24.020
This is proposition one in the paper, which models the human body as a mechanical plant.

21:24.720 --> 21:28.960
In control systems engineering, a plant is simply the physical system being controlled.

21:29.260 --> 21:33.520
It possesses inherent physical properties like mass, damping, and stiffness.

21:33.520 --> 21:39.520
When you hear a terrifying sound or you anticipate a loud impact, your immediate instinct is to brace for it.

21:40.000 --> 21:40.500
We tense up.

21:40.860 --> 21:41.620
We lock our muscles.

21:42.040 --> 21:42.200
Yeah.

21:42.380 --> 21:44.480
In biomechanics, this is called co-contraction.

21:45.240 --> 21:47.960
You simultaneously activate antagonist muscle groups.

21:48.220 --> 21:51.140
You fire your flexors and your extensors at the exact same time.

21:51.560 --> 21:54.760
The joint doesn't actually move, but the entire structure becomes rigid.

21:55.160 --> 21:57.840
You dramatically increase the stiffness of your mechanical plant.

21:58.560 --> 22:01.460
Instinctively, we brace because we feel like it protects us, right?

22:01.460 --> 22:03.880
We're turning ourselves into a harder target.

22:04.580 --> 22:10.700
But the mathematical equations Flyxion provides for this mechanical plant reveal something deeply counterintuitive about bracing.

22:11.100 --> 22:13.820
The math shows that bracing is a double-edged sword.

22:14.580 --> 22:21.560
If the incoming acoustic force is at a very low frequency, below the natural relaxed resonance frequency of your body,

22:21.560 --> 22:25.800
then tensing up does indeed reduce the amplitude of your bodily vibration.

22:26.020 --> 22:27.700
It basically acts as a shield.

22:28.320 --> 22:33.760
Okay, so if a slow, low rumble hits me, tensing my core stops my organs from shaking as much.

22:34.020 --> 22:34.440
Yes.

22:35.180 --> 22:42.020
But, depending on the specific ratio of stiffness you gain versus the damping you gain when you co-contract those muscles,

22:42.620 --> 22:49.320
bracing can actually amplify your bodily response to sounds that are in a specific frequency band above your natural resonance.

22:49.320 --> 22:54.320
Wait, really? Tensing up can actually make the sound vibrate my body more violently.

22:55.020 --> 23:00.720
Yes. By stiffening your musculature, you physically shift your body's natural resonance frequency higher.

23:01.260 --> 23:04.520
If you shift it closer to the frequency of the incoming sound wave,

23:04.760 --> 23:09.800
you enter resonance and you absorb far more energy than if you had just stayed completely relaxed.

23:10.960 --> 23:16.480
I was trying to wrap my head around this math, and I kept coming back to the suspension on a car.

23:16.860 --> 23:18.160
Oh, that's a good way to look at it.

23:18.160 --> 23:22.760
Imagine you were driving, and you see a massive, jagged pothole coming up in the road.

23:23.700 --> 23:24.580
You can't steer around it.

23:25.040 --> 23:30.420
If your car has adjustable suspension, and you hit a button to maximally stiffen the shocks right before impact,

23:30.840 --> 23:32.620
you aren't making the pothole disappear.

23:32.800 --> 23:34.420
No, the obstacle remains unchanged.

23:34.600 --> 23:34.840
Right.

23:35.480 --> 23:38.740
All you are doing is changing the transfer function of the chassis.

23:38.740 --> 23:46.660
You are changing the frequency at which that kinetic energy travels from the tire through the metal and rattles your teeth inside the cabin.

23:47.080 --> 23:55.140
And often, hitting a bump with a hyper-stiff suspension makes the jolt feel exponentially worse than if you had just left the suspension loose and compliant.

23:55.140 --> 23:57.620
That is a phenomenal analogy.

23:57.840 --> 23:58.120
It is.

23:58.640 --> 24:03.900
Voluntary bracing is not a magical volume knob that turns down the physical reality of the world.

24:04.100 --> 24:06.560
It merely alters your mechanical impedance.

24:07.060 --> 24:11.320
It dictates which frequencies will pass through you and which will be resistant.

24:11.320 --> 24:14.740
So, our physical mass regulates the immediate impact.

24:15.320 --> 24:18.820
But, how does our nervous system store the memory of this event?

24:19.660 --> 24:28.440
Because Flyxion aggressively pushes back against the traditional cognitive idea that acoustic meaning is stored as abstract, symbolic data in the brain.

24:28.940 --> 24:36.440
Yes, the paper totally rejects the idea that we have a mental dictionary where the sound of a screeching violin is simply mapped to a text file that says danger.

24:36.720 --> 24:36.980
Right.

24:36.980 --> 24:44.500
Instead, Flyxion argues that the meaning of the sound is stored directly within the physical tuning of opposing rhythmic units in our biology.

24:45.140 --> 24:46.560
Opposing rhythmic units.

24:47.160 --> 24:49.560
Are we talking about the flexors and extensors again?

24:50.060 --> 24:52.360
Yes, but at the neural control level.

24:53.040 --> 24:56.220
Flyxion utilizes the Matsuoka model of neural oscillators.

24:56.920 --> 25:01.260
Think about fundamental rhythmic human actions like walking or breathing or chewing.

25:01.260 --> 25:06.280
We don't consciously calculate the micro-contractions of every muscle fiber for every step.

25:07.000 --> 25:11.580
Evolution solved this with central pattern generators in the spinal cord and brainstem.

25:12.340 --> 25:14.400
Rhythmic circuits that just kind of run on autopilot.

25:14.740 --> 25:15.300
Exactly.

25:15.880 --> 25:18.700
And they operate on the principle of reciprocal innervation.

25:19.240 --> 25:29.040
When the motor neuron commanding your bicep to fire is activated, it simultaneously sends an inhibitory signal to the motor neuron commanding your tricep, forcing it to relax.

25:29.040 --> 25:38.320
Two mutually inhibiting neurons form what is known as a half-center, a biological tug-of-war between a tense state and a release state.

25:39.360 --> 25:42.280
So how does a horror movie soundtrack interact with a half-center?

25:42.280 --> 25:53.760
Flyxion proposes that we literally store our reaction to acoustic parameters like threat level, urgency, or intensity as tuning weights within these biological oscillators.

25:53.760 --> 26:02.260
When you hear the screeching violin, the acoustic force drives the tense pole of your muscular oscillators to dominate the released pole.

26:02.260 --> 26:05.100
The sound isn't translated into a symbol of danger.

26:05.680 --> 26:10.040
The physical state of muscular tension is the meaning of the sound.

26:10.640 --> 26:14.460
The storage and the expression are the exact same biological event.

26:14.660 --> 26:14.880
Yes.

26:14.880 --> 26:23.560
To prove how these opposing forces interact, the paper brings in the Haken-Kelso-Bunds model, the HKB model of coordination.

26:24.240 --> 26:26.380
And this part absolutely fascinated me.

26:27.000 --> 26:30.220
The HKB model is a cornerstone of dynamic systems theory.

26:30.580 --> 26:35.760
It mathematically describes how two-coupled oscillators behave when subjected to changing parameters.

26:36.160 --> 26:41.500
The most important concept it demonstrates is bistability and sudden phase transitions.

26:41.500 --> 26:47.340
The example in the literature that made this click for me is the finger tapping experiment, or foot tapping.

26:47.860 --> 26:50.720
If you're listening to a metronome, you have a choice.

26:51.480 --> 26:53.140
You can tap your foot exactly on the beat.

26:53.740 --> 26:55.900
You are joining the energy of the sound.

26:56.280 --> 26:57.780
This is called in-phase coordination.

26:58.440 --> 27:01.180
And it is the most stable state for the human motor system.

27:01.480 --> 27:07.800
But you can also choose to deliberately tap your foot exactly between the beats, on the syncopation.

27:08.140 --> 27:10.860
You are actively resisting the main energy pulse.

27:10.860 --> 27:12.600
This is anti-phase coordination.

27:13.520 --> 27:16.960
It requires more cognitive and physical effort, but it is a stable state.

27:17.160 --> 27:17.960
You can maintain it.

27:18.300 --> 27:18.420
Right.

27:18.580 --> 27:20.100
But here is the phase transition.

27:20.800 --> 27:29.780
If the researcher slowly turns up the speed of the metronome, making it faster and faster, your brain and body can maintain that anti-phase resistance for a while.

27:29.780 --> 27:37.900
But eventually, a critical speed threshold is crossed, and your motor system physically cannot maintain the resistance anymore.

27:38.320 --> 27:42.380
Your foot will involuntarily suddenly snap into tapping on the beat.

27:42.380 --> 27:44.700
It isn't a gradual drift.

27:45.000 --> 27:48.320
It is a violent, sudden snap from one state to another.

27:48.820 --> 27:50.640
That is by stability.

27:51.340 --> 27:54.780
You have two discrete modes of coordination within a continuous system.

27:55.220 --> 27:59.840
Flighted maps this HKB dynamic directly onto our physical stance toward sound.

27:59.840 --> 28:05.260
Are your internal oscillators flowing with the acoustic energy, or are they bracing against it?

28:05.380 --> 28:05.480
Right.

28:05.700 --> 28:12.840
When a horror movie score slowly builds in intensity, you might be actively bracing against it, holding your tension in anti-phase.

28:12.840 --> 28:19.120
But if the acoustic force crosses a critical threshold, like a massive jump scare, your system snaps.

28:19.580 --> 28:23.020
The bracing fails, and the sound forces you into a new physical state.

28:23.700 --> 28:31.240
But if the sound forces my muscles to tense, and those muscles send feedback to my brain, this triggers a massive logical problem.

28:32.180 --> 28:32.320
Right?

28:32.900 --> 28:37.700
Flighted outlines this in Proposition 3, Runaway Tension and Loop Game.

28:38.260 --> 28:42.160
Because we have all been taught the simple feedback story of anxiety.

28:42.840 --> 28:44.680
The traditional story states,

28:45.160 --> 28:47.900
A terrifying sound causes your muscles to tense up.

28:48.480 --> 28:55.500
The proprioceptive feedback from that muscle tension triggers your autonomic nervous system, causing your heart rate and adrenaline to spike.

28:56.040 --> 29:01.200
And that massive spike in autonomic arousal signals your muscles to tense up even harder.

29:01.200 --> 29:02.360
A perfect circle.

29:03.040 --> 29:04.680
The tension causes the arousal.

29:04.820 --> 29:06.320
The arousal causes the tension.

29:07.100 --> 29:08.780
So I have to ask the obvious question.

29:08.780 --> 29:13.800
If this simple feedback loop is true, why don't we continuously escalate?

29:14.280 --> 29:22.040
Like, if the loop endlessly feeds itself, why don't I spiral into a terminal panic attack and literally have a heart attack every time I watch an action movie?

29:22.040 --> 29:24.420
It's a profound question of system stability.

29:24.420 --> 29:27.060
Why doesn't the machine rip itself apart?

29:28.080 --> 29:33.680
Fly Exxon solves this by introducing the mathematical concept of loop gain, represented by the Greek letter mu.

29:34.240 --> 29:37.260
Loop gain is the multiplier of the feedback circuit.

29:37.260 --> 29:39.300
Let's break down the math of mu.

29:40.080 --> 29:40.340
Okay.

29:40.720 --> 29:46.900
So if the loop gain, mu, is mathematically less than 1, the system is fundamentally stable.

29:47.420 --> 29:51.920
It means that on each trip around the feedback loop, the signal is multiplied by a fraction.

29:52.720 --> 29:53.080
Okay.

29:53.260 --> 29:59.000
So the tension causes arousal, but slightly less arousal, which causes slightly less tension.

29:59.220 --> 30:06.020
It finds a ceiling, plateaus safely, and when the movie ends, the fractional multiplication dampens the system back to baseline.

30:06.020 --> 30:08.680
Because it's a fraction, it constantly decays.

30:09.100 --> 30:10.080
So we survive the movie.

30:11.100 --> 30:14.840
But what happens if the loop gain, mu, mathematically exceeds 1?

30:14.980 --> 30:19.960
If mu is greater than 1, the signal is amplified on every single pass.

30:20.500 --> 30:22.600
Is that when the system rips itself apart?

30:23.260 --> 30:27.020
Not entirely, because biological organisms have physical limits.

30:27.380 --> 30:29.000
Your muscles will eventually fatigue.

30:29.340 --> 30:30.940
Your adrenal glands will deplete.

30:31.260 --> 30:35.840
The oscillators have boundaries that prevent true infinite runaway.

30:36.020 --> 30:43.560
However, what does happen when mu exceeds 1 is that the system enters a mathematical state called bi-stability with hysteresis.

30:44.080 --> 30:44.600
Hysteresis.

30:45.220 --> 30:45.500
Okay.

30:46.140 --> 30:47.200
This is a crucial concept.

30:48.060 --> 30:50.900
How does hysteresis alter our physical experience of fear?

30:51.280 --> 30:57.220
Hysteresis means that the current state of a system is dependent on its history, not just its current inputs.

30:57.220 --> 30:59.480
Think about the trajectory of a scare.

31:00.060 --> 31:02.660
The movie soundtrack plays a low, ominous rumble.

31:03.360 --> 31:08.680
The volume slowly climbs from a 2 out of 10 to a 5 out of 10 to an 8 out of 10.

31:09.100 --> 31:13.320
As the volume climbs, your muscular tension and heart rate climb with it.

31:13.980 --> 31:14.760
That makes perfect sense.

31:15.520 --> 31:17.240
The force goes up, the tension goes up.

31:17.240 --> 31:19.660
But then, the scene resolves.

31:20.280 --> 31:22.320
And the rumble slowly fades back down.

31:22.960 --> 31:26.000
From an 8 to a 5, down to a 2.

31:26.560 --> 31:32.360
If the system was perfectly symmetrical, your body tension would drop in exact synchronization with the volume.

31:33.080 --> 31:38.220
But if the loop gain was greater than 1, you have triggered hysteresis.

31:38.220 --> 31:40.340
So, what happens on the way down?

31:41.060 --> 31:49.660
On the way down, when the volume hits a 5 out of 10, your bodily tension does not match the level it was at when the volume was a 5 out of 10 on the way up.

31:50.120 --> 31:53.160
Your tension remains abnormally, disproportionately high.

31:53.740 --> 31:56.200
The feedback loop got stuck in the upper state.

31:56.600 --> 32:01.680
It's like a biological thermostat that gets jammed on the heat setting even after the freezing wind is stopped.

32:01.940 --> 32:02.400
Exactly.

32:03.200 --> 32:05.040
You get trapped in the high-tension state.

32:05.040 --> 32:15.340
It requires a massive, prolonged drop in acoustic input, sometimes total silence, for the system to finally lose its grip and snap back down to the relaxed bass line.

32:15.900 --> 32:25.840
This perfectly explains why, 10 minutes after a massive jump scare, during a completely quiet dialogue scene, my shoulders are still up by my ears, and my heart is still pounding.

32:26.340 --> 32:27.400
The threat is gone.

32:27.940 --> 32:28.780
The sound is gone.

32:29.020 --> 32:30.920
But my system is trapped in hysteresis.

32:30.920 --> 32:35.580
The math perfectly maps onto the lived biological experience.

32:35.740 --> 32:38.540
Which brings us to a critical pivot in this deep dive.

32:39.020 --> 32:47.260
We have firmly established that our muscles and our nervous system are calculating, regulating, and storing our physical reaction to acoustic force.

32:48.000 --> 32:52.980
But everything we have discussed so far is a profoundly private, internal, biological calculation.

32:52.980 --> 32:58.260
How does this private biology scale up into a shared cultural experience?

32:58.820 --> 33:03.060
Like, how do entire societies learn the language of movie sound?

33:03.800 --> 33:08.380
To bridge that gap, we really have to bring vision and social observation into the framework.

33:08.740 --> 33:09.920
We don't just hear our environment.

33:10.080 --> 33:12.640
We watch it happen, and we watch others react to it.

33:13.060 --> 33:15.760
Flyxion explores this in the transition toward mimetics.

33:15.760 --> 33:20.760
Because seeing a physical action engages the exact same neural circuitry as hearing it, right?

33:21.180 --> 33:21.460
Yes.

33:22.220 --> 33:24.520
This introduces the concept of mirror neurons.

33:25.080 --> 33:30.880
Flyxion relies heavily on the transcranial magnetic stimulation, or TMS, studies conducted by FDIGA.

33:31.580 --> 33:36.040
Break down how TMS works in this context, because the methodology is wild.

33:36.040 --> 33:41.120
TMS involves placing an electromagnetic coil against the subject's skull.

33:41.460 --> 33:49.500
The coil delivers a targeted magnetic pulse that induces a weak electrical current in a highly specific region of the motor cortex beneath it.

33:49.940 --> 33:54.840
In FDIGA's studies, they found the exact spot in the brain that controls a muscle in the subject's hand.

33:55.660 --> 33:59.460
If they trigger the magnet, the subject's thumb involuntarily twitches.

33:59.820 --> 34:02.060
They can literally physically control the hand from the outside.

34:02.060 --> 34:09.540
Right. But the actual experiment involved having the subject just sit perfectly still and watch an experiment or grasp an object.

34:09.980 --> 34:15.460
As the subject watched the action, the researchers delivered a sub-threshold magnetic pulse.

34:15.880 --> 34:18.100
A pulse too weak to cause a twitch on its own.

34:18.380 --> 34:18.800
Oh, okay.

34:19.560 --> 34:27.980
But because the subject was watching a hand move, their own motor cortex was already so highly excited and primed by the visual information

34:27.980 --> 34:31.400
that the weak pulse suddenly caused the thumb to twitch violently.

34:31.400 --> 34:37.340
The visual observation literally brought the observer's physical motor system to the very edge of firing.

34:37.860 --> 34:41.740
Their body was silently preparing to copy the action just by looking at it.

34:42.260 --> 34:51.160
Now, as an impartial guide to this source material, we must explicitly state that Flyxion acknowledges the fierce, ongoing scientific debate surrounding mirror neurons.

34:51.420 --> 34:53.180
It is definitely not settled science.

34:53.380 --> 34:53.960
Far from it.

34:53.960 --> 35:03.800
Researchers like Hickok and Hayes have published extensive, devastating critiques of the idea that mirror neurons are the evolutionary foundation of action understanding.

35:04.680 --> 35:10.340
Furthermore, the paper notes the massive replication failures in psychology regarding the facial feedback hypothesis.

35:10.820 --> 35:10.940
Right.

35:10.940 --> 35:18.820
The famous idea that simply forcing your face into a smile will make you feel happier was utterly debunked by the Wagenmacher's replication study.

35:18.820 --> 35:27.840
So if the science of mirror neurons and physical feedback is so hotly debated, doesn't that cause Flyxion's entire model to kind of collapse?

35:27.840 --> 35:32.300
It would, except Flyxion makes a brilliant structural defense.

35:32.300 --> 35:39.820
The paper states that the mechanical and oscillatory model does not rely on why these sensory motor connections exist.

35:40.320 --> 35:46.520
It does not matter if mirror neurons are a grand, innate evolutionary adaptation we are born with,

35:46.860 --> 35:53.380
or if they are simply the result of associative Pavlovian learning over a lifetime of seeing and doing things simultaneously.

35:54.100 --> 35:56.080
So it doesn't matter if it's hardware or software.

35:56.080 --> 36:03.680
Exactly. The only thing that matters for the model is the empirical, measurable fact that the connection exists in adult humans.

36:04.160 --> 36:06.860
When you see an action, your motor system lights up.

36:07.500 --> 36:11.480
The routes are there, regardless of their evolutionary origin story.

36:12.020 --> 36:16.800
And because those overlapping routes exist between our auditory, visual, and motor systems,

36:17.560 --> 36:20.560
film sound designers can just mercilessly exploit them?

36:20.780 --> 36:22.580
Oh, they absolutely hack the system.

36:22.580 --> 36:29.160
The paper highlights a survey conducted by Blomstein regarding the use of non-linear analogs in cinema.

36:29.700 --> 36:33.200
First, what exactly defines a non-linear analog in acoustics?

36:33.560 --> 36:40.280
A non-linear analog is a sound that breaks the normal, linear, predictable rules of acoustic production.

36:41.020 --> 36:45.320
The best example in nature is an animal in a state of absolute existential terror.

36:45.320 --> 36:52.780
When a marmot or a primate screams because a predator has caught it, it pushes far too much air through its vocal cords.

36:53.260 --> 36:54.700
The physical system overloads.

36:55.220 --> 36:59.800
The resulting sound wave becomes harsh, noisy, chaotic, and abrupt.

36:59.940 --> 37:02.580
It literally tears the fabric of the sound.

37:03.060 --> 37:05.280
That's the sound of a biological system failing.

37:05.280 --> 37:05.900
Yes.

37:06.740 --> 37:12.560
Blomstein analyzed over a hundred iconic film soundtracks and discovered that sound designers routinely

37:12.560 --> 37:19.720
artificially mix these chaotic, non-linear animal distress frequencies directly into human screams.

37:20.120 --> 37:23.960
Or weave them subtly into the ambient orchestral music.

37:24.060 --> 37:27.680
Why go through the trouble of hiding animal distress calls in the score?

37:27.680 --> 37:36.940
Because, if the visual image of the killer on the screen is not sufficient to fully trigger the audience's deep brainstem fear response,

37:37.600 --> 37:43.840
injecting a non-linear analog into the audio track will artificially bypass the cognitive brain entirely.

37:44.360 --> 37:48.820
It directly attacks the acoustic startle reflex and forces the biological alarm to ring.

37:48.820 --> 37:56.700
They are injecting mathematical acoustic chaos into the theater to physically force our muscles to co-contract.

37:56.700 --> 38:02.300
We think we are scared of the movie, but we're actually just being mechanically stimulated by distress frequencies.

38:02.960 --> 38:05.600
Which brings us to the core philosophical shift of the paper.

38:06.600 --> 38:11.860
How does this biological manipulation transition from mimetic to mimetic?

38:12.920 --> 38:19.140
So, we spent the first half of this discussion on mimesis, imitation happening within a single individual.

38:19.640 --> 38:22.980
But Richard Dawkins famously coined the term MEM-8.

38:22.980 --> 38:28.800
We use the word MEM-8 constantly for internet jokes, but Dawkins' original definition is far more profound, right?

38:29.340 --> 38:35.140
He derived MEM-8 from the Greek word mimeme, which means a unit of imitation.

38:36.080 --> 38:40.500
Dawkins proposed that just as genes propagate biological data through reproduction,

38:41.460 --> 38:45.960
memes propagate cultural data by jumping from brain to brain through imitation.

38:45.960 --> 38:51.020
And Flyxion maps this concept of the MEM-8 directly onto bodily tension.

38:51.680 --> 38:57.540
But to make this work, the paper makes a crucial distinction about why groups of people react similarly to sound.

38:58.080 --> 39:03.100
We have to separate common exposure convergence from true cultural copying.

39:03.760 --> 39:05.000
And this is a vital nuance.

39:05.540 --> 39:11.720
Common exposure convergence simply means that a group of people all react the exact same way to a loud, harsh explosion,

39:11.720 --> 39:15.680
because they all share the identical human biological hardware.

39:16.080 --> 39:20.260
They all have the same cochlea, the same brainstem reflexes, and the same startle pathways.

39:20.440 --> 39:24.680
Okay, if a massive clap of thunder hits, an entire crowd will flinch at the same time.

39:25.160 --> 39:27.360
But I didn't learn to flinch by watching you flinch.

39:27.800 --> 39:30.380
We just both possess the same acoustic startle reflex.

39:30.760 --> 39:31.200
Exactly.

39:31.780 --> 39:33.260
Convergence is not memetics.

39:33.260 --> 39:41.980
True memetic transmission, copying, occurs when one individual learns a specific mapping between a sound and a physical tension state

39:41.980 --> 39:44.880
by visually observing another person's physical reaction.

39:45.720 --> 39:46.680
Okay, let's play this out.

39:47.460 --> 39:48.520
Imagine I am a toddler.

39:49.100 --> 39:54.360
I am in the house and I hear a very specific low-frequency hum from a new machine in the basement.

39:54.720 --> 39:57.380
I have no biological reflex for this sound.

39:57.700 --> 39:59.440
I don't know if it's dangerous or safe.

39:59.720 --> 40:00.000
Right.

40:00.220 --> 40:01.720
But I look across the room at my mother.

40:01.720 --> 40:08.540
I see her jaw clench tightly, her shoulders rise into a defensive posture, and her breathing becomes shallow.

40:09.440 --> 40:14.360
Through those visual motor pathways we discussed, I internally copy her physical tension state.

40:14.840 --> 40:21.320
And in that moment, my nervous system permanently maps that specific hum to that specific state of muscular tension.

40:21.620 --> 40:23.480
You have just downloaded a cultural MEM-8.

40:24.000 --> 40:28.300
The physical tension itself becomes the private interlanguage between you and your mother.

40:28.300 --> 40:31.800
You do not need to possess the vocabulary word for danger.

40:32.240 --> 40:35.840
The physical state of being clenched is the entire meaning of the sound.

40:36.260 --> 40:40.740
And it was transmitted entirely through visual observation of an internal bodily state.

40:41.120 --> 40:41.720
That is staggering.

40:42.340 --> 40:44.800
The tension is the carrier wave for the meaning.

40:45.300 --> 40:49.600
But is there empirical evidence that this kind of mapping transfers across radically different cultures?

40:49.600 --> 40:50.920
Or is it localized?

40:51.640 --> 40:55.820
Flyxion brings in a few critical cross-cultural and cross-species studies here.

40:56.380 --> 41:00.960
First, there is Morton's work on motivation structural rules in animal acoustics.

41:01.620 --> 41:11.580
Morton demonstrated that across a vast array of bird and mammal species, low, harsh, nonlinear sounds universally signify hostility or aggression.

41:11.580 --> 41:16.160
While high, tonal, pure sounds signify appeasement or fear.

41:16.740 --> 41:19.940
The difference between a deep growl and a high-pitched whimper.

41:20.260 --> 41:22.500
It's universally understood across species boundaries.

41:22.620 --> 41:22.740
Right.

41:23.260 --> 41:33.740
But to test this in humans, the paper highlights a remarkable study that compared the emotional reactions of Canadian listeners with Mbenzelae pygmy listeners from the Congo Basin.

41:34.220 --> 41:37.740
The researchers played the exact same musical excerpts to both groups.

41:37.740 --> 41:41.060
Two groups with zero overlapping cultural or musical history.

41:41.580 --> 41:43.420
Did they react the same way to the sounds?

41:44.100 --> 41:45.780
The results were beautifully split.

41:46.500 --> 41:54.760
The researchers found that the axis of valence, meaning whether the listener interpreted the music as subjectively happy or sad, did not transfer well at all.

41:54.980 --> 41:56.760
It was highly culturally dependent.

41:57.480 --> 42:01.140
So a major chord doesn't automatically mean happy to a pygmy listener?

42:01.600 --> 42:02.020
Correct.

42:02.020 --> 42:16.820
But the axis of intensity, whether the music drove the listener into a state of high arousal and clenched tension or a state of calm, unclenched release, transferred almost universally between the Canadians and the Mbenzelae pygmies.

42:16.820 --> 42:17.300
Wow.

42:17.940 --> 42:22.820
So the concepts of happy and sad are constructed cultural conventions that we have to be taught.

42:23.340 --> 42:28.980
But the states of clenched and unclenched form a universal biological language that supersedes culture?

42:29.380 --> 42:31.640
That is Flyxion's grand hypothesis.

42:32.600 --> 42:37.660
Bodily tension is the universal biological ledger on which all acoustic meaning is written and transmitted.

42:37.660 --> 42:40.980
This is all incredibly mind-expanding theory.

42:41.340 --> 42:46.000
The physics of base, the brainstem reflexes, the oscillator math, the cultural mimetics.

42:46.580 --> 42:54.140
But as we both know, an ambitious theoretical model like this is effectively just poetry, unless it can be rigorously proven in a laboratory.

42:54.660 --> 42:55.120
Absolutely.

42:55.560 --> 42:57.580
A scientific model must be falsifiable.

42:58.060 --> 43:01.900
It must make specific predictions that can be tested and potentially proven wrong.

43:02.320 --> 43:04.060
And Flyxion embraces this completely.

43:04.060 --> 43:14.160
The paper concludes with an entire section dedicated to empiricism, proposing seven highly detailed specific experimental designs to test the mechanics of this framework.

43:14.800 --> 43:19.040
They give future researchers the exact blueprints on how to destroy the theory.

43:19.560 --> 43:25.480
Let's break down the most critical experiments, starting with experiment one, sound and picture fully crossed.

43:26.200 --> 43:30.800
This experiment is designed to test the mute button paradox in a controlled biometric setting.

43:31.040 --> 43:31.860
How do they set it up?

43:31.860 --> 43:39.240
The researchers would wire subjects to monitors measuring electromyography, muscle tension, galvanic skin response, and heart rate.

43:40.000 --> 43:45.560
They show the subjects a terrifying horror sequence, but they fully cross the audio-visual conditions.

43:45.980 --> 43:47.080
Okay, and what are the conditions?

43:47.340 --> 43:50.720
In condition one, they watch the scene with the original terrifying soundtrack.

43:51.340 --> 43:53.820
In condition two, the sound is heavily attenuated.

43:54.140 --> 43:55.560
The volume is dialed way down.

43:55.560 --> 44:03.400
In condition three, all the abrupt, nonlinear, transient effects are digitally scrubbed out, but the dialogue and ambient room noise are kept.

44:03.900 --> 44:08.320
And in condition four, they watch the visual sequence in total dead silence.

44:08.320 --> 44:12.360
And what specific biological marker are they looking for to prove the theory?

44:12.820 --> 44:15.120
They are tracking the habituation of anticipation.

44:15.540 --> 44:27.480
The goal is to see if the physiological anticipation, the sweaty palms and clenched neck before the monster jumps, remains high upon repeated viewings, even as the raw brainstem startle reflex habituates and fades away.

44:27.760 --> 44:29.780
And what would prove Flyxion wrong?

44:29.780 --> 44:45.320
If the subject's physiological tension response remains identical, regardless of whether the sound is at 100% volume or 10% volume, then Flyxion's core thesis, that sound is a literal mechanical force pushing on the body, is completely falsified.

44:45.620 --> 44:51.540
If the volume doesn't matter, then the brain is merely decoding abstract information and the physical force theory collapses.

44:51.920 --> 44:54.540
They have to prove that the wave itself is doing the heavy lifting.

44:54.880 --> 44:55.340
I love it.

44:56.120 --> 44:58.120
What about testing the mechanical plant?

44:58.120 --> 45:04.300
The idea that tensing up changes our resonant frequency, like the stiff suspension hitting a pothole.

45:04.400 --> 45:09.420
That is handled in experiments two and three, using impedance probes and physical transducers.

45:09.700 --> 45:11.040
Paint the picture of experiment three.

45:11.560 --> 45:17.220
In experiment three, the researchers need to isolate structure-borne vibration from airborne vibration.

45:17.740 --> 45:27.240
They strap a subject into a specialized chair equipped with a seat transducer, a device that physically vibrates the chair and the subject's skeleton without producing much sound in the air.

45:27.240 --> 45:34.340
They run a low-frequency sweep through the chair, and then they run the exact same frequency sweep through massive loudspeakers in the air.

45:34.700 --> 45:38.200
To see if the body treats bone rattling differently than air pressure rattling.

45:38.820 --> 45:41.780
And experiment two tests the co-contraction math.

45:42.020 --> 45:42.260
Yes.

45:42.620 --> 45:47.200
In experiment two, they place a mechanical impedance probe directly on the subject's chest.

45:47.200 --> 45:52.960
They instruct the subject to intentionally brace their trunk, to co-contract their flexors and extensors.

45:53.680 --> 46:01.540
The probe measures if the physical resonance frequency of the chest cavity mathematically shifts, exactly as Proposition 1 predicts when the stiffness is altered.

46:02.260 --> 46:08.380
If the subject braces and the resonance frequency does not shift upward, the mechanical plant model is entirely wrong.

46:08.380 --> 46:10.240
It is so cleanly testable.

46:11.000 --> 46:12.100
What about the cultural side?

46:12.220 --> 46:15.920
How do they test if memes are literally transmitted through visible muscle tension?

46:16.140 --> 46:18.620
That is experiment five, diffusion chains.

46:19.280 --> 46:22.140
This is designed to track memetic decay over time.

46:22.340 --> 46:23.940
It's like scientific game of telephone.

46:24.460 --> 46:25.460
Exactly like telephone.

46:26.000 --> 46:27.220
You set up a chain of participants.

46:27.800 --> 46:32.640
Person A is exposed to a novel, ambiguous sound, while person B observes their physical reaction.

46:33.340 --> 46:36.800
Person B is then exposed to the sound, while person C observes them.

46:36.800 --> 46:39.800
This continues down a chain of 10 or 20 people.

46:40.180 --> 46:43.520
And they are trying to see which part of the reaction survives the copying process?

46:44.000 --> 46:44.480
Precisely.

46:45.100 --> 46:50.400
They track both the intensity mapping, whether the subject adopts a clenched or unclenched physical state,

46:50.640 --> 46:55.220
and the valence mapping, whether the subject verbally reports the sound as happy or sad.

46:55.880 --> 47:01.020
The theory predicts that the physical intensity mapping will survive intact down the entire chain,

47:01.140 --> 47:04.320
while the valence mapping will degrade, mutate, or vanish.

47:04.320 --> 47:08.060
But if the happy, sad meaning survives the telephone game perfectly,

47:08.660 --> 47:12.000
or if the physical tension mapping gets lost after the third person,

47:12.460 --> 47:15.600
the memetic hypothesis of bodily tension is proven false.

47:16.140 --> 47:16.440
Correct.

47:16.820 --> 47:17.020
Okay.

47:17.280 --> 47:19.840
I have one more experiment I want to highlight,

47:20.260 --> 47:24.480
because we talked extensively about the sticky thermostat, the hysteresis.

47:24.940 --> 47:29.260
The idea that loop gain exceeds one, and our fear gets trapped.

47:30.000 --> 47:31.760
How do they test that mathematically?

47:31.760 --> 47:36.180
This is a beautiful, elegant test built into the latter half of experiment three.

47:36.640 --> 47:41.620
The researchers expose the subject to a low-frequency, ominous acoustic rumble.

47:41.940 --> 47:47.000
They slowly, continuously turn the volume up, measuring the subject's rising muscular tension.

47:47.240 --> 47:48.600
Volume goes up, tension goes up.

47:48.860 --> 47:53.740
Then, without stopping, they slowly and continuously turn the volume back down to silence.

47:54.040 --> 47:55.920
And they are looking for asymmetry.

47:55.920 --> 48:02.900
If loop gain, mu, is greater than 1, the subject's bodily tension on the way down should stay abnormally high,

48:03.120 --> 48:07.260
even when the volume drops to the exact same decibel level it was at on the way up.

48:07.460 --> 48:07.720
Yes.

48:08.100 --> 48:12.560
If the volume hits 60 decibels on the ascent, the tension might be relatively low.

48:12.760 --> 48:17.580
But when the volume drops back down to 60 decibels on the descent, the tension should still be peaking.

48:17.580 --> 48:24.460
But if the tension perfectly mirrors the volume, if it goes up and comes down in perfect, symmetrical synchronization,

48:24.800 --> 48:30.640
then there is no hysteresis, the loop gain is under 1, and the model of trapped fear is wrong.

48:30.900 --> 48:33.740
It is a perfect test of biological memory.

48:33.920 --> 48:35.140
It is absolutely brilliant.

48:35.780 --> 48:38.460
What we have covered in immense, complex landscape today.

48:38.780 --> 48:41.340
Let's synthesize this journey before we go.

48:41.340 --> 48:46.720
If we pull all these threads together, Flyxion's paper forces a paradigm shift.

48:47.240 --> 48:52.040
Sound is not merely abstract information waiting to be neutrally decoded by a rational brain.

48:52.540 --> 48:58.580
It is an active, physical, mechanical force that pushes against our tissue and our skeleton.

48:59.240 --> 49:04.380
Our biology desperately regulates this force through unconscious muscular co-contraction.

49:04.380 --> 49:12.240
By anticipating visual cues and by storing the acoustic memory directly within rhythmic, biological oscillators in our nervous system.

49:12.760 --> 49:15.940
The constant tug-of-war between tense and released states.

49:16.180 --> 49:16.500
Exactly.

49:17.060 --> 49:24.540
And because we visually observe these tension states in each other, this internal physical tuning can be copied from person to person.

49:25.140 --> 49:31.980
It forms a foundational, universal layer of human culture that operates entirely below the level of conscious language.

49:31.980 --> 49:40.880
So, to you, the listener, the next time you sit down in a theater or feel the bass at a concert or even just hear a sudden siren blast on the street,

49:41.500 --> 49:44.180
I want you to actively pay attention to your own physical mass.

49:45.120 --> 49:48.840
Notice how your muscles are acting as a literal, physical filter for the world.

49:49.740 --> 49:56.220
Notice the undeniable delay between your shoulders clenching and your cognitive brain finally realizing what the sound actually was.

49:56.780 --> 50:00.140
You are a mechanical plant constantly regulating force.

50:00.140 --> 50:04.400
You are experiencing physics long before you ever experience meaning.

50:05.700 --> 50:09.420
And I want to leave you with one final, slightly provocative thought.

50:09.520 --> 50:18.940
Something to mull over that isn't explicitly spelled out in the paper, but is a massive, inevitable implication of everything we just talked about regarding hysteresis and memetics.

50:19.300 --> 50:21.540
If we extrapolate the math to the real world.

50:21.940 --> 50:22.320
Exactly.

50:22.320 --> 50:32.740
If Flyxion is right, if our bodies constantly, physically map sound attention, and if a loop gain over one means our fear thermostat gets stuck in a high state,

50:33.160 --> 50:36.680
what does that mean for the physical environments we have constructed for ourselves?

50:37.060 --> 50:39.780
The modern industrialized environment.

50:39.780 --> 50:42.620
Think about our modern architecture.

50:43.540 --> 50:45.800
The screeching wheels of the subway systems.

50:46.380 --> 50:52.740
The constant, droning, low-frequency hum of massive HVAC systems in our open plan offices.

50:53.300 --> 50:57.420
The rumble of highway traffic passing through concrete canyons.

50:57.420 --> 51:09.560
If sound is a physical force that directly modulates the oscillator storage in our musculature, what if the spaces we live in are acting as massive, invisible sound design engines?

51:09.980 --> 51:13.640
Functioning exactly like the horror movie soundtracks Bloomstein analyzed.

51:13.980 --> 51:14.340
Completely.

51:15.060 --> 51:22.340
Could the ambient, low-frequency drones of modern cities be slowly, perpetually programming our collective muscular loop gain?

51:22.340 --> 51:32.620
Are we shifting our entire societal tension levels upward, constantly getting stuck in systemic hysteresis, without us ever consciously identifying the source of our anxiety?

51:33.000 --> 51:39.380
Are our cities acting like a massive, terrifying movie soundtrack that we simply cannot mute?

51:40.080 --> 51:41.360
It is a staggering thought.

51:41.360 --> 51:57.740
If the brainstem startle reflex is gain-controlled by our underlying state, and the industrialized environment is constantly providing a low-level, unyielding mechanical force, we might be existing in a society-wide state of perpetual bracing.

51:58.080 --> 52:04.700
We are all driving with perpetually stiffened suspensions, just absorbing every single pothole at maximum force.

52:04.700 --> 52:12.560
So the next time your heart races during a horror movie, just remember, at least in the theater, you actually know why you're scared.

52:13.300 --> 52:16.320
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the source material.

52:16.840 --> 52:17.820
We will see you next time.

